#
# The USB keyboard and mouse at boot: cadr-usb-input reading the board's own
# USB host port and sending what it finds to cadr-terminal, which writes the
# I/O board's registers.
#
# S88, after S85cadr-terminal, because the terminal is what listens on the
# link.  Started before it, this program says the link is not there and tries
# again every second, so the order is tidiness rather than a dependency --- but
# a console log that reads in the order the machine needs things is worth the
# number.
#
# **NO OPTION IS PASSED AND NONE IS WANTED.**  The program looks in
# /dev/input for a keyboard and a mouse, opens what is there, and keeps
# looking, so one init script is right for a board with a keyboard plugged in,
# one with a keyboard plugged in later, and one with nothing plugged in at
# all.  Its flags are in docs/usb-input.md, and they are named so that they
# can move into the card's `fpgarc` beside the Chaosnet's when that file comes
# to serve more than one program.
#
# **THE DEVICES ARE NOT TAKEN EXCLUSIVELY.**  `--grab` would keep the keys from
# anything else on the board, and nothing else on this board reads them: there
# is no virtual console, the login is on the serial line, and the keyboard is
# there to type at the CADR.  Off by default all the same, because a grab that
# succeeds on a device somebody is debugging with `evtest` is a keyboard that
# has silently stopped answering them.
#
# The log goes to the console, which is where the board is watched from
# (docs/boot.md).  start-stop-daemon's -b would send it to /dev/null, so the
# program is told where to write instead.

PIDFILE=/var/run/cadr-usb-input.pid

start() {
	printf "Starting cadr-usb-input: "
	start-stop-daemon -S -q -b -m -p "$PIDFILE" \
		--exec /usr/bin/cadr-usb-input -- --log /dev/console
	[ $? = 0 ] && echo "OK" || echo "FAIL"
}

stop() {
	printf "Stopping cadr-usb-input: "
	start-stop-daemon -K -q -p "$PIDFILE"
	[ $? = 0 ] && echo "OK" || echo "FAIL"
}

case "$1" in
	start) start ;;
	stop) stop ;;
	restart|reload) stop; start ;;
	*) echo "Usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}"; exit 1 ;;
esac

exit $?
